6. Capture-flow UX
Arabic-first screen title: مسح علبة الدواء; English localization: Scan medicine box.
Guide the user through front → back → side 1 → side 2 → optional flap. Let them label/relabel a captured image or mark its side type as unknown; do not assume the first photo is always the front.
Allow 1–6 photos. Show thumbnails and allow retake, delete, and review of each captured side before processing.
Main action: تم — استخراج البيانات / Done — extract data.
Show brief guidance: good lighting, hold steady, fill the frame, avoid glare, and capture a close-up of faint batch/expiry stamps.
Provide flash toggle and tap-to-focus where hardware and package support them.
Request camera access only on entry. Handle denied, permanently denied, restricted, unavailable-camera, capture-error, and cancellation states with a clear localized message and safe recovery path.
Correctly dispose of the camera controller and temporary files on navigation away, errors, retakes, deletion, and cancellation.
Use proper Arabic RTL layout. Latin strengths, batch codes, and dates must display left-to-right within RTL text using Directionality and/or Unicode isolation as appropriate.
7. OCR pipeline for every photo
Load safely: verify the image can be decoded; apply EXIF orientation and normalize coordinate tracking.
Preprocess conservatively: resize the longest edge to roughly 1600 px while preserving legibility; use grayscale, contrast adjustment, and light sharpening. Keep the original available for retries. Do not apply processing that deletes small text.
Faint stamped text: when needed, create a second adaptive-threshold/stronger-contrast variant for ink-jet or embossed batch/expiry marks. Treat it as an additional candidate source, not automatically as better evidence.
Rotation retry: if the first pass recognizes little text, retry 90°, 180°, and 270° as useful. Choose by a consistent measure such as valid text/keyword evidence, not only raw character count. Maintain mapping from transformed bounding boxes back to the displayed source image.
OCR selection: use the Latin engine for Latin text and run Arabic-capable OCR when Arabic characters are present or the Latin result is sparse. For Tesseract, use the bundled ara+eng models. Avoid unnecessary duplicate full-resolution passes.
Preserve output: keep line/block ordering, boxes, available confidence, engine/source identifier, and the raw recognized text for each side.
Responsiveness: report progress by side and phase. Do heavy Dart processing off the UI isolate where supported. Target under roughly 3 seconds per side on a mid-range phone as a goal, not a correctness guarantee; slow devices must remain usable and cancellable.
8. Text normalization
Implement and test these rules:

Convert Arabic-Indic digits ٠١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨٩ and Persian digits ۰۱۲۳۴۵۶۷۸۹ to Western digits 0123456789 for parsing, while preserving the original recognized text for display.
Normalize common Unicode dash, slash, dot, degree, micro-unit, and whitespace variants where safe.
Collapse repeated whitespace and normalize case only for comparison, not for the user-visible original value.
Apply OCR substitutions such as O → 0, I/l → 1, S → 5, and B → 8 only inside a validated numeric/date context. Never alter ordinary medicine names or ingredient words using these substitutions.
For batch codes, normalize surrounding spaces and case for comparison, but preserve the original candidate for display. Do not remove meaningful internal characters unless the normalization rule is explicitly tested.
Do not infer missing characters or silently complete truncated values.
9. Field extraction rules
Use multilingual keyword/context matching plus format validation. Keep field-specific rules in one testable layer. A value without sufficient contextual or format evidence should be rejected or left as a low-confidence alternative.

Expiry date
Keywords include EXP, Exp., Expiry, Exp. Date, Use before, Use by, انتهاء, تاريخ الانتهاء, صالح حتى, and ينتهي.

Support these printed formats when they can be parsed unambiguously:

MM/YYYY, MM-YY, MM.YYYY
DD/MM/YYYY
YYYY-MM, YYYY/MM/DD
MMM YYYY (for example, JAN 2027), MMM-YY
Recognized Arabic month names (including variants such as يناير and كانون الثاني) when the month/year pairing is clear.
Rules:

For month-only expiry dates, represent the expiry as the last calendar day of that month.
Apply a documented two-digit-year rule. Reject implausible/far-future interpretations rather than silently selecting a questionable century.
Reject invalid calendar dates and expiry dates more than 10 years ahead of the current date. Inject the current date/clock where needed so tests are deterministic.
Prefer a date on the same line as an expiry keyword, then a nearby line immediately below it. Distance to the keyword is evidence, not proof.
Do not extract a bare date as expiry with high confidence when no expiry label or convincing context is present.
If two different expiry candidates have similar confidence, leave the selected expiry field empty and show both candidates. Do not choose one just because it appeared on the front/back first.
If the selected expiry is already past, use the established SafetyStrings.expiredDisposal warning and require explicit user confirmation regardless of confidence.
Manufacturing date
Keywords include MFG, MFD, Mfg. Date, Prod., Manufactured, تاريخ الإنتاج, and تاريخ التصنيع.

Apply the same date-format validation as expiry.
A manufacturing date should precede the expiry date. If it does not, reduce confidence and present the conflicting candidates instead of silently correcting either date.
Detect a common stamped block such as LOT: AB123 / MFG: 01/2025 / EXP: 12/2027. Extract the three labeled values from the same block as a related group and give the group a modest evidence bonus. Do not let that bonus override invalid formats or a clear conflict.
Batch/lot number
Keywords include LOT, Lot No, Batch, B.No, Batch No, رقم التشغيلة, and التشغيلة.

Candidate token length: 3–20 alphanumeric characters, allowing only separators the existing model can preserve safely.
Prefer the token immediately after a batch/lot label.
Do not treat a date, long registration number, barcode-adjacent number, phone number, or price as a batch number.
If no label or strong context exists, leave the field empty rather than guessing.
Strength
Recognize number-plus-unit patterns such as 500 mg, 250mg/5ml, 1%, 100000 IU, and common Arabic unit spellings including ملغ, مجم, مغ, and مل.

Preserve meaningful concentration expressions such as mg/ml, mg/5ml, and mcg/µg. Do not confuse pack volume with dose strength without surrounding context.

Dosage form
Recognize common labels such as tablets/أقراص, capsules/كبسولات, syrup/شراب, cream/كريم, ointment/مرهم, spray/بخاخ, drops/قطرات, and injection/vial/ampoule/حقن.

Map only to values supported by the existing MedForm model. Do not collapse ointment into cream unless the existing app deliberately models them as the same form. If the model cannot represent an extracted form accurately, leave the typed field unset (or use an existing explicit other value) and retain the OCR candidate for user review. Never invent an enum member.

Pack quantity/size
Recognize patterns such as 20 tablets, 30 caps, 100 ml, 20 قرص, 30 كبسولة, and عبوة 30. Keep pack quantity/size distinct from strength and from the number of individual doses.

Trade/brand name
Prefer the prominent text on a photo explicitly labeled as the front of the package.
Use text-block height/prominence as one signal, not the only signal. Exclude obvious strengths, manufacturer names, generic phrases, and directions such as tablets or for oral use.
A repeated matching value across different sides increases confidence modestly.
If Arabic and Latin names are clearly presented as a pair on the same side, keep the pair or select the representation the existing model supports. Do not invent a translation.
Active ingredient
Look near Each tablet contains, Each capsule contains, Composition, Active ingredient, يحتوي كل قرص على, المادة الفعالة, and التركيب. The line below the trade name may provide a clue, but position alone is insufficient. Extract only text that is visibly recognized; do not search the internet or infer an ingredient from a brand name.

Manufacturer
Look near Manufactured by, Mfd. by, Marketed by, Made in, تصنيع, إنتاج, الشركة المصنعة, or a company-like line ending with Co., Ltd, Pharma, Pharmaceuticals, or شركة. Company suffixes alone are not enough to classify a line with high confidence.

Storage conditions
Recognize text such as Store at 2–8°C, Keep refrigerated, يحفظ في الثلاجة, and يحفظ في درجة حرارة 2–8 as evidence for a refrigeration suggestion, which the user must confirm.

Store below 25°C does not by itself mean the medicine needs refrigeration.
Do not freeze does not by itself mean refrigeration is required.
Preserve a storage-condition hint only if the existing app has an appropriate field or review-only display. Do not add unsupported medical/storage fields without checking the existing schema.
10. Multi-side merging and confidence
Collect candidates from all sides and OCR passes, preserving provenance.
Normalize candidates before comparing them, but keep the original text for display.
When the OCR engine exposes a confidence value, use it as one evidence input. Combine it with the parser's evidence (valid format, matching nearby keyword, correct field context, bounding-box/layout signal, and repeated agreement) using documented constants in one place.
When engine confidence is unavailable, use a conservative rule-based estimate and label it as an estimated confidence, not a calibrated probability. Do not display a green/high-confidence state from a fabricated OCR score.
Repeated identical normalized values across independent sides may gain up to +0.15 per additional side, capped at 0.95. Centralize this rule and test it.
Default threshold for populating the draft: 0.60, configurable in one place. Candidates below threshold stay empty but may be shown in alternatives/raw text.
If two different values have similar confidence (start with a configurable tie margin such as 0.10), do not select either automatically. Leave the chosen field empty and show both candidates, especially for expiry and manufacturing dates.
Repeated evidence must never override an invalid date, a missing mandatory label, an unsupported enum value, or a clear safety conflict.
Do not turn the scores into medical certainty. The review UI must communicate that values are extracted suggestions and can be wrong.
11. Review screen and transition to the existing form
Each supported field should show:

The proposed value, or an explicit empty state.
A confidence indicator: green for >= 0.85, amber for 0.60–0.849, and no confidence dot below 0.60; label the score as estimated where applicable.
The source side thumbnail and, where accurate coordinates are available, tap-to-open/zoom to the source bounding box.
Alternative candidates as selectable chips when there are competing readings.
A way to edit, clear, and explicitly confirm a proposed value.
Include:

عرض النص المستخرج / Show recognized text, which displays the raw text for each side for transparency.
متابعة إلى نموذج الإضافة / Continue to add form, which passes the reviewed MedicineDraft to the existing AddMedicineScreen(prefill: draft) route/constructor.
An explicit expiry-confirmation action. Do not make the expiry field count as confirmed merely because the user pressed the general continue button.
The existing expired warning when the chosen expiry is in the past.
Do not add a second medicine-save mechanism to the scan flow. The final create/save action remains the existing add-medicine form's responsibility. Ensure a manually entered medicine can still be created without using the scanner.

